This operation itself is always safe, but using the resulting pointer is not."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The resulting pointer \"remembers\" the [allocation] that `self` points to; it must not"},{"DocComment":" be used to read or write other allocations."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" In other words, `let z = x.wrapping_add((y as usize) - (x as usize))` does *not* make `z`"},{"DocComment":" the same as `y` even if we assume `T` has size `1` and there is no overflow: `z` is still"},{"DocComment":" attached to the object `x` is attached to, and dereferencing it is Undefined Behavior unless"},{"DocComment":" `x` and `y` point into the same allocation."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Compared to [`add`], this method basically delays the requirement of staying within the"},{"DocComment":" same allocation: [`add`] is immediate Undefined Behavior when crossing object"},{"DocComment":" boundaries; `wrapping_add` produces a pointer but still leads to Undefined Behavior if a"},{"DocComment":" pointer is dereferenced when it is out-of-bounds of the object it is attached to. [`add`]"},{"DocComment":" can be optimized better and is thus preferable in performance-sensitive code."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The delayed check only considers the value of the pointer that was dereferenced, not the"},{"DocComment":" intermediate values used during the computation of the final result. For example,"},{"DocComment":" `x.wrapping_add(o).wrapping_sub(o)` is always the same as `x`. In other words, leaving the"},{"DocComment":" allocation and then re-entering it later is permitted."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`add`]: #method.add"},{"DocComment":" [allocation]: crate::ptr#allocation"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Examples"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" // Iterate using a raw pointer in increments of two elements"},{"DocComment":" let data = [1u8, 2, 3, 4, 5];"},{"DocComment":" let mut ptr: *const u8 = data.as_ptr();"},{"DocC